Unix utility 'sudo'

The Unix utility 'sudo' runs a single command as the root user, without opening a session
as root the way 'su' does. The command and whatever arguments follow it are passed through
untouched, and the identity goes back to the original user as soon as the command ends.

Authorization comes from /etc/sudoers, which holds one numeric user id per line. Blank lines
and lines starting with # are ignored. The id checked is that of the real user who ran
'sudo', not the one it runs the command under, so marking the utility setuid does not let
anyone past the list. A missing or empty /etc/sudoers authorizes nobody.

For this to work at all, /sbin/sudo itself has to be marked setuid, which the 'protect'
utility does at boot from the list in /etc/protect.

USAGE:

sudo <command> [arguments]

Example of use:

sudo mount hd1p0 /mnt

It mounts the volume as root, from a session of a common user listed in /etc/sudoers.

For more information, enter 'sudo ?' or 'sudo --help'.

